## 3. Android and Google Play compatibility

Inspect the current Android SDK configuration and prepare the project for the applicable Play submission requirements at the time of release.

For an ordinary Android phone/tablet app, verify that the release targets Android 16 (API 36) or higher as required for new submissions and updates from August 31, 2026.

Requirements:

- Set compatible `compileSdk` and `targetSdk` values.
- Check the installed SDK and Gradle/Android Gradle Plugin compatibility before upgrading.
- Upgrade dependencies only when necessary.
- Do not arbitrarily increase `minSdk` and exclude existing users.
- Review Android 13+ notification permission behavior.
- Verify edge-to-edge behavior and system-bar insets on newer Android versions.
- Verify notification and alarm behavior against the targeted Android versions.
- Check package visibility, exported components, intent filters, and pending-intent mutability.
- Check whether any deprecated API use creates a release or security problem.
- Resolve manifest merge conflicts.
- Preserve existing functionality on supported older Android versions.
- Document any incompatibility that cannot be safely resolved.

Do not blindly upgrade every dependency to its latest version. Choose compatible stable versions and verify the result by building and testing.

## 4. Production application configuration

Prepare a clean release configuration.

### Application identity

- Preserve the existing application ID unless there is a confirmed reason to change it.
- Ensure the displayed app name is consistently `CancelBefore`.
- Confirm the version name and monotonically increasing version code.
- Remove development-only labels, fake production data, debug-only navigation, and placeholder text from release builds.
- Ensure release configuration does not use debug signing.
- Verify the app does not rely on machine-specific local paths or credentials.
- Confirm the release build can be reproduced from documented instructions.

Never change the application ID casually: it affects the identity of the Play Store app.

### Build variants and secrets

- Separate debug and release configuration.
- Ensure debug logging and verbose diagnostics are disabled or safely redacted in production.
- Ensure API keys, OAuth secrets, keystore passwords, and private configuration are not committed.
- Use environment variables or untracked local configuration for release signing credentials.
- Add or update `.gitignore` for local secrets, signing files, build outputs, IDE state, and temporary data as appropriate.
- Do not place private client secrets in the Android app. Values embedded in an APK or AAB must be treated as extractable.
- Inspect the final release manifest and packaged resources for debug configuration and unintended permissions.

If signing credentials are missing, document the steps needed to create or supply them securely. Never generate a fake release signature or report a signed release artifact that was not produced.

## 5. Permission and policy audit

Review every permission in the manifest and justify it against a user-visible feature.

### Notifications

- Request `POST_NOTIFICATIONS` at an appropriate point in the user journey on Android 13+.
- Explain the benefit before asking.
- Handle denial and subsequent permission changes gracefully.
- Show the current notification capability in settings or diagnostics.
- Never imply a reminder will definitely appear if notifications are disabled.

### Notification access

If `NotificationDetectionService` is present:

- Keep scanning opt-in.
- Explain that the service can inspect notifications from other apps.
- Explain why this is necessary and what CancelBefore processes.
- Do not imply universal detection across all payment providers.
- Handle disabled access, revocation, and service reconnection.
- Avoid collecting or transmitting unrelated notification content.
- Keep sensitive content out of logs.
- Ensure the feature remains disabled until the user explicitly enables it.

### Exact alarms

Audit the existing `SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM` or `USE_EXACT_ALARM` implementation.

- Do not add `USE_EXACT_ALARM` merely to avoid asking the user for access.
- Evaluate the appropriate permission based on the app's actual core functionality and current Play policy.
- Prefer inexact alarms where acceptable.
- If precise reminder timing is genuinely necessary, explain it clearly and handle special-access denial or revocation.
- Check `canScheduleExactAlarms()` before scheduling exact alarms.
- Provide a fallback when permission is unavailable.
- Verify reminders are rescheduled correctly after permission changes, device reboot, app update, clock change, and timezone change.
- Never claim that exact delivery is guaranteed under all Android power-management conditions.

### Other permissions and components

- Remove unnecessary permissions.
- Do not add SMS, contacts, Accessibility, location, or broad storage permissions unless a genuine implemented feature requires them and their policy requirements are satisfied.
- Review every exported service, receiver, activity, and provider.
- Ensure exported components validate external input and do not expose internal data.
- Verify FileProvider paths are restrictive.
- Verify URI handling and imported files are validated.
- Do not introduce a foreground service simply to keep a scanner running continuously.

For every permission retained, record its purpose, when it is used, and how denial is handled.

## 6. Privacy, data safety, and user trust

CancelBefore deals with subscription information, payment amounts, notifications, and potentially email content. Privacy disclosures must accurately describe the actual implementation.

Inspect the entire codebase and all SDKs for data collection, network requests, analytics, crash reporting, advertising, and third-party transmission.

Create or update `docs/PRIVACY_POLICY.md` and any appropriate public-facing privacy-policy draft.

The privacy policy must accurately address:

- Information entered by users.
- Subscription amounts, merchant names, dates, notes, and reminders.
- Notification content accessed when the optional scanner is enabled.
- Gmail access and extracted information, but only if Gmail integration actually exists.
- What is stored locally.
- Whether any data leaves the device.
- Data retention and deletion.
- Backup/export behavior.
- Third-party SDKs and service providers actually used.
- Security practices and their real limitations.
- Contact information supplied by the developer.
- Policy updates and an appropriate effective date.

Do not invent a company address, support email, legal entity, or security certification. If required details are unavailable, leave clear placeholders in the documentation and list them as launch blockers.

### Privacy implementation

- Do not log payment details, raw notification text, email bodies, OAuth tokens, or private identifiers.
- Avoid sending sensitive data to analytics or crash-reporting services.
- Verify local data deletion behavior.
- Verify that clearing data cancels pending reminders and alarms.
- Inspect Android backup rules so that sensitive data is not unexpectedly copied or exposed.
- Make backup/export behavior clear to users.
- Ensure imported JSON and exported CSV are validated and handled safely.
- If Gmail exists, ensure disconnect removes Gmail credentials and Gmail-specific cached data while preserving unrelated subscriptions.
- Ensure the privacy policy is consistent with the actual network behavior and third-party SDK configuration.

### Google Play Data safety

Prepare a draft Data safety declaration based on actual source-code behavior, SDKs, and data flows.

Document, for each relevant data category:

- Whether it is collected.
- Whether it is shared.
- Purpose of use.
- Whether processing is optional.
- Retention and deletion behavior.
- Whether processing occurs on-device only or involves transmission.

Do not automatically declare that the app collects no data just because its main database is local. Verify all SDKs and network requests.

The final Data safety form must be completed by the developer in Play Console using the verified implementation details.

### Account deletion

Inspect whether the app actually creates an application account.

If it does, implement and document the account deletion functionality required by current Google Play policy, including an appropriate in-app deletion path and an external deletion-request path where required.

If the app does not create an account, do not invent a meaningless account-deletion feature. Still provide an understandable way to clear local data and disconnect optional integrations.

